{
 "lead": "A closed system can follow many paths between two states. One family obeys pV^n = C, where p is pressure in kPa, V is volume in m³, n is the polytropic index, and C is a constant. The value of n fixes the process and gives ready formulas for work and heat. Compressors, engine cylinders and gas pipelines use these paths. This chapter integrates p dV once and then reads isothermal, adiabatic, isobaric and isochoric processes as special cases.",
 "prereq": "You need: the first law Q − W = ΔU from <a href=\"#first-law-for-closed-systems-cycle-and-process\">Chapter 2.1</a>, internal energy depending on T from <a href=\"#internal-energy-and-enthalpy\">Chapter 2.2</a>, and γ = c_p/c_v from <a href=\"#specific-heats-of-gases\">Chapter 2.3</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "One family of paths",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A process is a change from state 1 to state 2. A polytropic process follows pV^n = C. Here p is pressure, V is volume, n is the polytropic index (a pure number), and C is a constant. For an ideal gas pV = mRT, so the path also fixes T."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The index selects familiar processes: n = 0 gives constant p (isobaric); n = 1 gives constant T (isothermal, because pV is constant); n = γ gives adiabatic; n → ∞ gives constant V (isochoric). Here γ = c_p/c_v. Sign convention: W is work done by the gas and Q is heat added to the gas. Compression makes V₂ < V₁ and W negative. The area under the p-V path is W."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "n",
      "Process",
      "Path",
      "Work W",
      "Heat Q"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "0",
       "Isobaric",
       "p = C",
       "p(V₂ − V₁)",
       "m c_p ΔT"
      ],
      [
       "1",
       "Isothermal",
       "pV = C",
       "mRT ln(V₂/V₁)",
       "W (ideal gas)"
      ],
      [
       "γ",
       "Adiabatic",
       "pV^γ = C",
       "m c_v(T₁ − T₂)",
       "0"
      ],
      [
       "→ ∞",
       "Isochoric",
       "V = C",
       "0",
       "m c_v ΔT"
      ]
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Work from the integral",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Displacement work is W = ∫p dV from V₁ to V₂. Put p = C/V^n and integrate."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "W = ∫ from V₁ to V₂ p dV = (p₁V₁ − p₂V₂)/(n − 1) = mR(T₁ − T₂)/(n − 1),  n ≠ 1",
     "where": "p is pressure in kPa; V is volume in m³; T is temperature in K; m is mass in kg; R is the gas constant in kJ/kg·K; n is the polytropic index; state 1 is start and state 2 is end. W is positive when the gas does work."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For n = 1 the denominator is zero, so use the log form:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "W = p₁V₁ ln(V₂/V₁) = mRT ln(V₂/V₁),  n = 1",
     "where": "ln is the natural logarithm; for an ideal gas at constant T, ΔU = 0, so Q = W."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In isothermal compression, V₂ < V₁, ln(V₂/V₁) is negative, W is negative, and Q is negative: work in, heat out."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Adiabatic and polytropic",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Adiabatic means Q = 0. For an ideal gas with constant specific heats, pV^γ = C. The gas relations are:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "T₂/T₁ = (V₁/V₂)^(γ−1) = (p₂/p₁)^((γ−1)/γ)",
     "where": "γ = c_p/c_v is the ratio of specific heats; the volume ratio is inverted because V falls when T rises in compression."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Work is W = (p₁V₁ − p₂V₂)/(γ − 1) = m c_v (T₁ − T₂). Since Q = 0, ΔU = −W. In compression W < 0, so ΔU > 0 and T rises. For a general polytropic path:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Q = m c_n (T₂ − T₁),   c_n = c_v (n − γ)/(n − 1)",
     "where": "c_n is the polytropic specific heat in kJ/kg·K; use this only for n ≠ 1; c_v is the specific heat at constant volume in kJ/kg·K."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here c_n is a path-specific heat, not a property. Check limits: n = γ gives c_n = 0 (Q = 0); n = 0 gives c_n = c_p; n → ∞ gives c_n = c_v. For n = 1 use the isothermal formula. On a p-V diagram, p = C/V^γ falls more steeply than p = C/V because γ > 1. Flow versions are in <a href=\"#steady-flow-energy-equation-and-its-applications\">Chapter 2.6</a>."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
